# Line endings are checked in as LF, always. The project is developed on Windows,
# where Git would otherwise store whatever the working copy has, and the build
# then breaks on Linux in ways that are tedious to diagnose: F-Droid runs both
# `gradlew` and `native/build-linux.sh`, and a CR at the end of the shebang makes
# the kernel look for an interpreter named "/bin/sh\r". The `.patch` files fail
# the same way, since `patch` treats the CR as part of the context line.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-only, and only correct with CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Patches are compared byte for byte against a pinned checksum, so nothing may
# touch them. `-whitespace` also keeps Git from flagging the trailing space that
# a legitimately empty context line consists of.
*.patch -text -whitespace

# Binary: never converted, never diffed as text.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.webp binary
*.ttf binary
*.jar binary
*.aar binary
*.so binary
*.keystore binary
*.jks binary
gradle/wrapper/gradle-wrapper.jar binary
